---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import snapshot from '../../../public/demo/robotdata-studio/data/manifest.json';
---
<BaseLayout title="RobotData Studio — Suhang Xia" description="An independent local-first workspace for robot data catalogs, quality, provenance and versioned selections.">
 <article class="rds-project section-shell">
  <a class="text-link" href="/research/">← Research</a><p class="eyebrow">Independent software · Robot data infrastructure</p>
  <h1>RobotData Studio</h1><p class="rds-lead">From collected files to an inspectable data asset.</p>
  <p>Robot demonstrations carry more than actions: sensor timing, calibration, processing history and decisions about what to train on. RobotData Studio brings these records into one local workspace, while a browser demo makes a fixed public catalog directly queryable.</p>
  <div class="rds-links"><a href="/demo/robotdata-studio/?dataset=tactile-umi" class="rds-open">Open interactive demo ↗</a><a class="text-link" href="/demo/robotdata-studio/?dataset=tactile-umi&view=schema">Explore the schema →</a></div>
  <figure><img src="/data/robotdata-studio-desktop.png" alt="Actual RobotData Studio Explorer displaying real Tactile UMI episodes with filters and linked representations" width="1440" height="1000"/><figcaption>Real catalog, real queries. Public snapshot {snapshot.snapshot_id}.</figcaption></figure>
  <h2>One core. Two ways to inspect.</h2>
  <div class="rds-architecture" aria-label="Architecture: H5, Zarr and LeRobot sources enter the Python core; versioned Parquet serves local DuckDB and Spark as well as browser DuckDB-Wasm"><span>H5 · Zarr · LeRobot</span><b>→</b><span>Python Core<br/><small>profiles · checks · lineage</small></span><b>→</b><span>Versioned Parquet</span><b>→</b><span>Local workspace<br/><small>DuckDB · Spark</small><hr/>Public browser<br/><small>DuckDB-Wasm</small></span></div>
  <p>The local application registers read-only sources, scans fields and timing, records review history, and freezes catalog or selection manifests. SQLite tracks operational state; immutable Parquet snapshots hold analytical results. Spark performs batch joins and aggregates; DuckDB handles interactive queries.</p>
  <p>The public demo shares the React workspace and queries exported Parquet in a Web Worker. Visitors can filter episodes, inspect physical field meanings and quality findings, follow artifact bindings, and download a personal selection draft. It runs without a connection to the collection computer.</p>
  <h2>Connected to actual robot data.</h2>
  <p>The current catalog contains {snapshot.counts.episodes} logical episodes across Tactile UMI insertion demonstrations and a DROID LeRobot v3 export. Raw H5, aligned H5 and training Zarr remain linked representations of the same UMI episodes. Imported historical analyses are distinguished from checks executed by this software.</p>
  <details><summary>Methods and current boundaries</summary><p>Six Spark SQL recipes were compared against DuckDB on the real snapshot. Timestamp diagnostics, mapped-age intervals and source-frame reuse describe observable data conditions, not task success or labeling accuracy. Full media decoding remains unrun unless explicitly performed. Frozen manifests preserve selection and provenance; they are not complete LeRobot or Zarr training-data conversions.</p><p>RobotData Studio is independent of Tactile UMI, UniForce and Diffusion Policy. Existing Quest and camera latency work follows the credited exUMI method; this catalog does not claim that method as a contribution.</p></details>
 </article>
</BaseLayout>
